course: language
lesson: 10
title: Plurals that make no sense: mice, sheep and geese
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Most English plurals just add -s or -es (cat, cats; box, boxes), but some very old words change the vowel inside instead: mouse, mice; goose, geese; foot, feet; tooth, teeth; man, men.
explain: That is why "two hice" sounds silly: house took the ordinary -s long ago, while mouse kept its old Old English plural; it is history, not logic.
explain: Some words don't change at all: one sheep, two sheep; one deer, two deer; one moose, two moose; you know how many from the number or from "is" and "are".
explain: Where a word comes from decides a lot: moose came from a Native American (Algonquian) language and mongoose from Marathi, a language of India, so neither has anything to do with goose and neither becomes "meese" or "mongeese".
explain: Plurals never need an apostrophe: a shop sign "Tomatoe's 50p" is wrong; it should be "Tomatoes 50p".
fact: An irregular plural is a plural not made by adding -s or -es; old vowel-change plurals include mice, lice, geese, feet, teeth, men and women.
fact: Sheep, deer and moose are the same in singular and plural; fish is usually the same too (three fish).
fact: Fishes is used, especially by scientists, for different kinds of fish: "the fishes of the Atlantic".
fact: Children is a double plural: Old English already had a plural "cildru", and later the old ending -en was added; oxen keeps the same -en ending.
fact: Octopus comes from Greek; octopuses is the usual English plural, octopodes is the Greek-style one, and octopi is common and listed in many dictionaries though it is built on a mistake.
fact: Many words ending in -o add -es (potatoes, tomatoes, heroes, echoes), but some just add -s (pianos, photos, zoos).
fact: An apostrophe shows missing letters or belonging, never just a plural.
fact: Dice was first the plural of "die"; today many people also say "a dice" for one.
fact: Mongoose comes from Marathi, a language of India, so its plural is mongooses.
fact: Human comes from Latin "humanus" through French and is not the English word "man", so its plural is humans.
fact: In the west of Scotland weans are children, and bairns are children in Scotland and the North East of England; "baltic" is Scottish slang for freezing cold, and "oot" means out.
fact: Trousers, scissors, glasses and pliers are plural-only nouns: one object with two matching parts, counted with "a pair of".
fact: Most nouns ending in a consonant plus y change to -ies (baby, babies), and some ending in -f change to -ves (leaf, leaves).
exercise: Why is it two mice but two houses? | Mouse kept an old vowel-change plural; house took -s | Mice is a spelling mistake that stuck and was never fixed | Houses are bigger than mice | Hice was banned by teachers
exercise: Which is an old vowel-change plural? | teeth | tooths | toothes | teethes
exercise: Mia counts "two sheeps". What is right? | two sheep | two sheepes | two shoop | two sheepies
exercise: Harry tries "two meese". What is the plural of moose? | moose | meese | mooses | moosen
exercise: Why didn't moose get a goose-geese plural? | It came from a Native American language, not Old English | Moose are far too big for the old goose-geese rule to fit | Canadians changed the rule | It did, "meese" is correct
exercise: Hamza says "two childs". What is right and why is it odd? | Children, a double plural with an old -en ending | Childs, because it just adds -s | Childer, from French | Childrens, the double plural
exercise: Which word keeps the same old -en plural ending? | oxen | kitten | garden | golden
exercise: Callum writes "two octopi". What is the usual English plural? | octopuses | octopussies | octopoda | octopee
exercise: A sign says "Tomatoe's 50p". What should it say? | Tomatoes 50p | Tomato's 50p | Tomatos' 50p | Tomatoe 50p
exercise: Which pair is correct? | potatoes and pianos | potatos and pianoes | potatoes and pianoes | potatos and pianos
exercise: Ellie asks for the plural of mongoose. What is it? | mongooses | mongeese | mongoose | mongeeses
exercise: Lucy's grandad says "many fishes in the sea". When is "fishes" used? | For different kinds of fish | For exactly two fish | Only for goldfish | Never, it is always wrong
exercise: Ruby asks why it's humans, not "humen". What is true? | Human comes from Latin, not from the English word man | Humen was the old form that died out a few hundred years ago | Humans is American and humen British | Man and human are the same word
exercise: A Scottish lad says "Bring the weans in, it's baltic oot there!" What does he mean? | Bring the children in, it's freezing out | Bring the wine in, it's far too sunny and hot out | Bring the wheels in, it's windy out | Bring the dogs in, it's raining out
exercise: Lily asks if "a trouser" is one leg. How do we count trousers? | a pair of trousers | a trouser | two trouser | one trousers piece
exercise: What are the plurals of baby and leaf? | babies and leaves | babys and leafs | babyes and leafes | babies and leafs
exercise: Which list has only correct plurals? | feet, deer, boxes, women | foots, deers, boxs, womans | feet, deers, boxes, womens | feets, deer, boxes, women
exercise: A riddle: "My singular and plural are the same, I live in woods, and I am not a sheep." Which word fits? | deer | goose | mouse | fox
test: Your mate goes "it's one die, two dice, so one mice, two mouse, easy." What's right? | One mouse, two mice | He's right, one mice, two mouse | One mouse, two mouses | One mice, two mices
test: A Geordie nan says "Get the bairns their tea, pet." ("Pet" is a friendly word like love; "tea" here is the evening meal.) Who gets fed? | The children | The pets | The neighbours | The baby animals
test: Your little brother insists "two gooses" is fine because "two mongooses" is. What's true? | Goose is old English and changes to geese; mongoose is from Marathi | He's right, both words just add -es like every other word | Both should change to -geese | Neither word has a plural
test: What would happen if every English plural just added -s? | We'd say mouses, foots and childs, and the old forms would disappear | Nothing, they already do | We'd have no plurals at all | Every word would end in -en
test: A Gen Z shop sign says "Fresh bagel's, they slay." ("Slay" means are amazing.) What's the fix? | Fresh bagels, they slay | Fresh bagel's', they slay | Fresh bagels's, they slay | Fresh bagel's is correct
test: In a cartoon, a fisherman shouts "I caught five fishes!" holding five cod. What's the normal way to say it? | I caught five fish | I caught five fishies | I caught five fishs | It's perfect, fishes is the only plural
test: Your cousin says octopi is the "real Latin" plural. What's the catch? | Octopus is Greek, so octopi is built on a mistake, though dictionaries accept it | He's right, octopus is Latin | Octopi is the only plural in dictionaries | The plural is octopus-es-i
test: A Glaswegian dad says "Two weans and a dug, that's me." ("Dug" is dog in Scots.) How many children? | Two | One | Three | None
test: Your sister writes "There are three deers in the park." Fix it. | There are three deer in the park | There are three deeres in the park | There are three dear in the park | There is three deer in the park
test: Mouse, mice. A louse is a tiny insect that can live in hair. Using the same old pattern, what is its plural? | lice | louses | lousen | leese
test: A puzzle: in a made-up language the plural of "pod" is "peed" and of "tok" is "teek". What is the plural of "lob"? | leeb | lobs | lobben | lib
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the plural "mice"? | It preserves an ancient pattern in which the vowel within the word is altered | It constitutes a spelling error subsequently accepted | It is formed by the regular addition of "-s" | It is borrowed from the French language
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the plural of "mongoose" is not "mongeese". | The word derives from Marathi and bears no relation to the English word "goose" | The animal is too small to merit an irregular plural | The form "mongeese" is reserved for formal registers | The word possesses no plural form whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the correctly punctuated notice. | Tomatoes 50p | Tomatoe's 50p | Tomato's 50p | Tomatoes' 50p
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the plural "children". | It combines an Old English plural with a further plural ending in "-en" | It is formed by the regular addition of "-s" | It is unrelated to the word "child" | It is a modern invention of the twentieth century
